   "name": "Mass, Weight & Gravitational Field Strength",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Mass, Weight & Gravitational Field Strength"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Mass"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Mass **is the measure of the amount of matter in an object\n\n  - Consequently, this is the property of an object that resists change in motion\n- The greater the mass of a body, the smaller the change produced by an applied force\n\n  - The SI unit for mass is the **kilogram** (kg)"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Weight"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Weight **is the force a body experiences due to being in a gravitational field, it is given by the equation"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "W = mg"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where\n\n  - m = mass (kg)\n  - g = gravitational field strength (N kg<sup>−1</sup>)"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Gravitational Field Strength"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Gravitational field strength is the force per kilogram that acts on an object, it is found using the equation"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "g = $\\frac{F}{m}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where\n\n  - F = weight (N)\n  - m = mass (kg)\n- On the Earth's surface the average gravitational field strength =** 9.81 N kg**<sup>**−1**</sup>"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Constant Acceleration in Freefall"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Freefall **is used to describe falling objects where the only force is weight\n\n  - Drag forces are ignored\n  - By considering freefall we can see that all objects must fall with exactly the same value for acceleration, regardless of their mass or weight"
  },
